 overlap we  coalesce them they group up into these  clusters and visually we put a count on  there so that your user can kind of see  you know that this is an important  cluster or not so we actually change the  size of the cluster we put numbers  around it and so on this is part of an  open-source library and I'll tell you  about that library in a second so you  can customize all of this let me show  you how much code is required to  implement marker clustering in your apps  the first thing you need to do is tell  your Gradle in your Gradle configuration  specify that you want to use the Android  Maps you tools library the second thing  you need to do is look at your data  class and in our case our data class is  going to be our list of restaurants  right so we'll have restaurants with  name and lat long and whatever else  maybe it'll be rating cost and any any  number of things but all we need to do  is add this one implement this interface  is one extra method called get position  which just returns its latitude  longitude once we have that done we  create or instantiate one of these  cluster managers and we tell our map  that any time the camera changes or the  user clicks on the map that they should  use that that the map should let the  cluster manager know about it and that's  how when we interact with the map those  clusters are like coalescing and forming  new ones or splitting up and then step  four we go ahead and tell our cluster  manager but here are all our restaurants  I didn't put the step 5 on there but the  step 5 is that you should go ahead and  remove any code that you have to add  markers onto the map or take markers are  out of the map because the cluster  manager is going to do that for you so  in these sort of 10 to 15 lines of code  we suddenly implemented cluster marker  marker clustering and we've probably  reduced the amount of code that we had  from before we started so great user  experience with minimal effort from a  development standpoint  instead of mark instead of clusters we  can also do heat maps and the heat map  implementation looks similar to clusters  where we generate these heat maps  depending on zoom levels and so as we  zoom in more and more you start to see  the heat map sort of start to split  apart or when we zoom out it combines  into larger ones now I mention this is  an open source library so if you don't  like my alarming colors you can  definitely change that the API itself  allows you to change that as well but  it's it's very easy to do again the  implementation for this is trivial the  same library that we use we add that  that interface we have on our data items  we then instantiate a heat map tile  provider this is the thing that's going  to generate the tiles depending on zoom  levels and lat Long's on that step 2 I  just wanted to point out the dot data  that's where we add our restaurants  right it's just that one line it's going  to manage it for us and then step three  we add a tile overlay and we add it to  the map itself that's it that's the  whole code for the heat map  implementation so when it comes to doing  deep integration really look at things  like custom styling personalize your  Maps look at ways that you can uniquely  present whatever data it is that you  want to present on a map don't overwhelm  your users use the visual metaphors  where possible all right so we've gotten  to the point where we've done some  integration but I've had developers come  and tell me but anchor I just want the  raw data you know what I will manage  everything I want to do it I want to  control everything I want to be the  orchestrator or all of this so let's  look at an example in sleep NASA  tracking right in typically with sleet  and NASA tracking we have a number of  vehicles we need to do some deliveries  we need to optimize around that there's  a number of api's that can really help  here the first is the directions API now  the directions API is a web service  right so you can call it on any platform  but we do we do recommend that you proxy  it any of our web services through a  server rather than consume them directly  on mobile and if you want to know more  about why that is we had a  talk today called the development best  practices for google maps of web  services it will be up on YouTube within  a day or so so please do look that up we  cover some great best practices there  back to the directions API the great  thing about the directions apos one that  returns you this nice JSON output it  tells you step-by-step directions on how  to get somewhere it tells you exactly  attitude and longitudes on how to get  your users to places you can specify  different travel modes whether you're  driving so in your in the use case we  were talking about the asset tracker it  might be a driving delivery that we're  doing or might be cycling we could do  that too but at the bottom there you'll  see duration we can actually give you  travel times of how long it's going to  take so either to complete this journey  and that's immensely powerful and very  very useful for things like our ride  sharing service that we talked about  earlier or even any delivery service  that we're going to do now this  information is powered by the same  engine that powers Google Maps a dozen  Avigail that provides real-time  directions we can take this one step  further let's say our business isn't  actually asking for this information  right now but they care about the  delivery time at 3am tomorrow morning we  have predictive travel times for that so  what we've done is over time we built  this hugely complicated models of  traffic for for all over the world we've  put it through a machine learning  algorithms and develop new machine  learned models out of that and we're  applying it to understand traffic  conditions not just now but when there  will be at various times of the week at  various points in the world and of  course if you know that your vehicle is  going to be on a road you should  consider using the roads API something  that actually surprises a lot of folks  is when they're told that the gps on  their phones regardless of which phone  you have it's actually not that accurate  right there are times unless there's a  perfect line of sight it's often not  very accurate it gives you a range of  values  and so even though you're driving along  a straight road you could get a GPS  response up here up here down there  across there I mean overall you're going  straight and you could kind of start to  see that pattern but the actual data  that it gives you is not necessarily  straight so I'm going to say computer  enhance and we're going to look at this  data that we recaptured so this is a car  that was traveling around this park and  around some buildings and what you can  see here is apparently this car cut  across a park drove through the park and  further down actually drove through a  building as well now you can judge my  driving however you want but I did not  do that the roads API can help us  eliminate these sorts of problems noisy  GPS so what we did is we then took those  exact same points put them through the  roads API and we ended up with the green  line and the roads API is intelligent  enough to know that hey look this is I  know that this vehicle is on a road  throughout this journey I'm going to  look at this data and I'm going to  interpolate what is actually happening  here and so will snap those points to  the road but when you've got like curved  roads and things as well it will  actually smooth that path out it will  add new points to that list of latitude  longitude so that when you plot it it  looks nice and smooth rather than a  jagged polygon so the roads API is  immensely useful let's look at local  discovery as a use case now in the case  of local discovery we just want to see  we want to learn about other places  around the particular location so I'm  from Sydney Australia and so when I  visit San Francisco one of the things  that I tend to do is hey which  restaurants should I go and check out  which bars you know what attractions  there are and one of the API is where we  provide this information in is the  places API and the places API is  something that we provide is a web  service as well as a native API on  Android and iOS I'm going to cover both  with the places API you can get lots of  information about a place because if you  think about a latitude and longitude  it's a point on earth it doesn't make a  lot of sense when you're in a  multi-story building in fact if you're  in a multi-story building like the  Moscone Center today you're not even  going to get a GPS location because we  don't have direct line of sight with the  satellites we're indoors we're also not  at a specific we're not just at a  latitude and longitude we have to  consider height and this is where places  can help places that is able to tell you  the difference between being on floor  three of this building versus floor too  if you're in a shopping mall you can  actually tell whether you're at a cafe  or a hairdresser even if there's  somewhere close by and the way it does  is is it does this is by using  information like latitude and longitudes  Bluetooth beacons Wi-Fi access points  and more as well as assisted GPS which  is triangulating cell phone tower  information as well but the great thing  is once you have a place it has a ton of  semantic information so here's a  response that we get from the Jason  request I've highlighted a couple of  things I'm just going to talk through  those the first one is formatted address  this is actually a big challenge to  solve as a developer because if you're a  delivery or a logistics company  especially a global one the way  addresses are formatted in different  parts of the world varies drastically  all right some places put have something  called a zip code some places have a  postcode some use numbers some use  letters some put the state before the  zip code some put them after and getting  that right can mean the difference  between your customer receiving their  package on time or either not getting it  or getting it late or not at all right  and the places API is able to format  this for you exactly to the destination  so we handle that complexity for you we  give you the latitude and longitude we  also give you the name but you're also  able to get some additional metadata you  can get photos of the place you can get  information like a ratings of what the  users have said about it you can get the  establishment type is it  the library's at a place of worship is  it a bar so there's tons of great  information and if you use the places  API in your apps the thing to remember  is to hold on to this thing at the  bottom called the place ID which is a  unique identifier for every place in the  world because once you have that place  ID you can retrieve any place details  you want so it's a great way to do a  local discovery up use the places API do  a place search and find what types of  places that are around and you can  filter by establishment types on the web  service for the Android and iOS API we  repurposed built the places API for  specific use cases so rather than  searching anywhere in the world we  wanted to make this a lot more  contextual so this is very much about  the places around you so in android we  call it get current place or current  place with call back on iOS and i'm  going to show you a quick demo what  happens is when when we use the sensors  on a phone we give you a list of places  that the user is likely to be at and  some values now you don't need to see  these values specifically clearly just  know that these likelihood values are  going to be between 0 and 1 and the  closer it is to one the more confident  we are that the user is at that place so  here's a demo we're driving along the  101 and you'll see that the list of  places that we're getting back is  changing the moving up and down and the  likelihood is changing as well as we  move along that route and that's where  you can really use this places API to  determine where a user is and so in that  location-sharing example we talked about  earlier I showed you the place picker  the get current place is the API that  powers the place picker so if you want  to orchestrate it yourself you can do  that it's a bit more work for you but  certainly gives you all the data and  information you need to make a good user  decision now we've just talked about a  number of web services today they're  really really useful in different  contexts we looked at some use cases we  looked at some solutions now you might  find that you need to use these web  services on different platforms and even  though web services can be consumed on  any platform we've tried to make that  development easier for you we've built a  number of client libraries  and we built them for go for Python for  node.js and for java and they're open  sourced they're available at github com  / google maps and not only do they make  it easier to consume these web services  on in those languages but we've actually  also put in some additional features all  right we've made authentication really  really easy we've added exponential  back-off for when you have any failures  we've also made it so that you can call  that api synchronously or asynchronously  depending on whatever style of  development you're using so it's worth  checking these up so that brings me to  the close of this presentation we've  talked about these four layers of  development delegation incorporation  integration and orchestration we've  looked at them in the context of various  use cases to build great solutions  everything I've talked about today is  actually available on  developers.google.com / maps so we're  all about developer documentation lives  and it links out to things like a github  repository as well so please do check it